{"subjectId":"sql","id":"like-patterns","title":"LIKE & Pattern Matching","group":"Querying Basics","order":13,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>LIKE</code> matches text against a simple pattern with two wildcards; regular expressions are available as dialect extensions.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Wildcard</th><th>Matches</th><th>Example</th><th>Matches…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>Any sequence of 0+ characters</td><td><code>'A%'</code></td><td>Asha, Arjun</td></tr><tr><td><code>_</code></td><td>Exactly one character</td><td><code>'_a%'</code></td><td>Ravi, Karan, Sara (second letter a)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name FROM employees WHERE name LIKE 'A%'   ORDER BY name;\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE name LIKE '_a%'  ORDER BY name;\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE name LIKE '%an%' ORDER BY name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n-----\nArjun\nAsha\n\nname\n-----\nKaran\nRavi\nSara\n\nname\n-----\nKaran</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-letter-case\"></i>Case sensitivity</h3>\n<p>SQLite's <code>LIKE</code> is case-insensitive for ASCII letters; PostgreSQL's <code>LIKE</code> is case-sensitive (use <code>ILIKE</code>); MySQL/SQL Server follow the column collation (usually insensitive). Portable trick: compare lowercased values.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name FROM employees WHERE name LIKE 'a%' ORDER BY name;              -- SQLite: matches A\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE name GLOB 'a*' ORDER BY name;              -- GLOB: case-sensitive\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE lower(name) LIKE 'a%' ORDER BY name;       -- portable</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n-----\nArjun\nAsha\n\nname\n----\n\nname\n-----\nArjun\nAsha</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-percentage\"></i>Matching a literal % or _</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE promos (code TEXT);\nINSERT INTO promos VALUES ('SAVE10%'), ('SAVE10'), ('FLAT_50'), ('FLATX50');\n\nSELECT code FROM promos WHERE code LIKE '%\\%' ESCAPE '\\' ORDER BY code;\nSELECT code FROM promos WHERE code LIKE 'FLAT\\_%' ESCAPE '\\' ORDER BY code;\nSELECT code FROM promos WHERE code LIKE 'FLAT_%' ORDER BY code;          -- _ is a wildcard!</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>code\n-------\nSAVE10%\n\ncode\n-------\nFLAT_50\n\ncode\n-------\nFLATX50\nFLAT_50</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-regex\"></i>Regular expressions (dialects)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>PostgreSQL / MySQL / Oracle</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name FROM employees WHERE name ~ '^[AEIOU]';          -- PostgreSQL (~* = case-insensitive)\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE name REGEXP '^[AEIOU]';     -- MySQL\nSELECT name FROM employees WHERE REGEXP_LIKE(name, '^[AEIOU]');  -- Oracle / MySQL 8</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Performance</div><p>A leading wildcard (<code>LIKE '%son'</code>) cannot use a normal B-tree index — the engine must scan every row. <code>LIKE 'son%'</code> (prefix) can. For real text search use full-text indexes (<code>FULLTEXT</code>, PostgreSQL <code>tsvector</code>, SQLite FTS5).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Find names that start and end with a vowel.</summary><div><p>Portable: <code>WHERE lower(substr(name,1,1)) IN ('a','e','i','o','u') AND lower(substr(name,-1)) IN ('a','e','i','o','u')</code> (SQL Server/MySQL: <code>LEFT</code>/<code>RIGHT</code>). With regex: <code>name ~* '^[aeiou].*[aeiou]$'</code> (PostgreSQL) or <code>REGEXP '^[aeiou].*[aeiou]$'</code> (MySQL).</p></div></details></section>"}